  "text": "Question thirteen. Why is vectorization preferred over apply with a lambda? Multiply the column by point nine, and that's one call into compiled code. Apply calls your Python function once for every value. A million rows, a million calls. Same answer, very different cost. Use apply only when there's no vectorized way. Question fourteen. How do you apply a function to each row or column? Apply with axis zero runs it on each column. Axis one runs it on each row. Question fifteen. Map, apply, or data frame map? Series map works value by value on one column, from a function or a lookup. Apply works along an axis. And data frame map works on every cell. The last of the slow ones changes a frame's shape.\n\nQuestion sixteen. How do you reshape from wide to long? Melt. Each quarter's column turns into rows, with a quarter column and a sales column. Two customers by two quarters is four rows. Question seventeen. Pivot or pivot table? Pivot only reshapes, so every region and category pair must be unique. Here North and A appear twice, and pivot stops: index contains duplicate entries. Pivot table aggregates, like a spreadsheet. North and A sum to thirty, and fill value puts a zero in the empty cell.",
